      "**Pry → Brex.** Acquired for $90M in April 2022. This one is the most broken technically: pry.co deep links now 404 outright on brex.com. Pry's old Academy guides still rank in search — including their guide to calculating startup runway — and every one of those clicks is a dead end.",
      "**Causal → Lucanet.** Acquired October 2024. causal.app redirects to Lucanet's Extended Planning & Analysis page. If you loved Causal because it was spreadsheet-native and you could model something in an afternoon, you now arrive at enterprise consolidation software.",
      "**Mosaic → HiBob.** Acquired for around $35M. Cleanest handoff of the group — it at least redirects to a finance page — but Mosaic is now a module inside an HR suite.",
      "**Runway → lost its domain.** On 22 July 2026, runway.com was acquired by Runway AI, the video-generation company. Runway Financial now lives at cfo.ai. Every bookmark, every blog link, every founder typing \"runway\" into a search bar now lands on an AI video tool.",
      "If you want the longer version — each acquisition in order, with what every one of those redirects actually lands on today — we wrote it up as [a timeline of the whole category](/blog/startup-fpa-tools-acquired-timeline).",
      "## The part that actually matters",
      "It would be easy to read that list as consolidation. It isn't. It's a migration upmarket.",
      "Look at what the survivors sell today. Runway's pricing page publishes no prices, no free tier, and no self-serve signup — three demo-gated tiers, and their own paid landing pages segment audiences as 50–500, 500–1,500, and 1,500+ employees. Their competitor comparison pages target Anaplan, Workday, and Pigment. Jirav, the only tool of the group still operating independently, sells to accounting firms and CFO advisory practices, and its homepage does not use the words \"runway\" or \"burn rate\" anywhere.",
      "None of these companies did anything wrong. Enterprise FP&A is a better business than seed FP&A — bigger contracts, lower churn, buyers with budget. Every one of them followed the money, and the money was upmarket.",
      "But it leaves a specific person stranded: the founder with three employees, nine months of cash, and a board meeting in a fortnight. That founder cannot expense a $1,500/month seat. That founder cannot wait nine days for a demo call to find out whether they can afford to hire an engineer in September.",
      "That founder is back in a spreadsheet.",
      "## What a spreadsheet actually costs you",
      "Nothing is wrong with a spreadsheet. Most good financial models start as one. The problem is what a spreadsheet cannot tell you.",
      "A spreadsheet gives you a single line: we run out of money in month nine. It cannot tell you the odds. It cannot tell you that if your best month repeats you get to fourteen, and if your worst month repeats you get to six. Real runway is a distribution, not a date, and a single-point forecast hides exactly the risk you are trying to reason about.",
      "It also goes stale. The model you built in January reflected January. By the time a board member asks a hard question in August, you are reconstructing eight months of drift from memory.",
      "And it cannot answer the question founders actually ask, which is never \"what is my runway.\" It is always some version of: if we do this thing, what happens? Hire two engineers in September. Push the raise a quarter. Cut paid acquisition by 40%. Every one of those is a scenario, and each one is a fresh tab, a fresh set of formulas, and a fresh chance to break a reference.",
      "## What to do this week",
      "Whatever you pick, do these three things before your model gets any staler.",
      "**Get your data out, if you still can.** If you had Finmark, Pry, Causal, or Mosaic, export your historical actuals to CSV now. Historical monthly revenue, COGS, opex, payroll, and cash balance are the only things that are genuinely expensive to reconstruct. Assumptions you can rebuild in an hour; three years of actuals you cannot.",
      "**Separate actuals from assumptions.** The most common thing wrong with an inherited model is that measured numbers and guessed numbers live in the same cells with no way to tell them apart. Split them. Your actuals are facts; everything forward is an assumption with a confidence level. Any tool worth using should show you which is which.",
      "**Insist on a range, not a date.** If your next model tells you \"you have 7.8 months,\" ask what the tenth and ninetieth percentile look like. If the tool can't answer, it's giving you false precision — and false precision in a runway number is how founders end up starting a raise ninety days too late.",
      "## Where FounderConsole fits",
      "We built FounderConsole for the founder the rest of the market left behind.",
      "You upload a CSV of your monthly financials — or connect Stripe or QuickBooks — and get a runway distribution rather than a single date, with P10, P50 and P90 bands. You model scenarios directly: hire two senior engineers in September at $16k a month, and watch the curve bend. Metrics we cannot compute honestly from your data, we mark as estimates or show as N/A, rather than inventing a flattering number.",
      "There is a [free runway calculator](/tools/runway-calculator) with no signup, no demo gate, and no call — and a [free default-alive check](/default-alive) for when the question you are really asking is whether you reach profitability on the cash you already have. If either one tells you what you need to know and you never come back, that is a fine outcome.",
      "We are not going to promise we will never be acquired. Everyone on that list above probably made that promise. What we will say is that self-serve pricing and a free tier are a structural commitment, not a marketing one: a company that has gone upmarket cannot keep them and stay profitable. Watch what happens to the free tier. That is the honest signal.",
