      "## The Monte Carlo Approach",
      "Instead of calculating a single runway number, sophisticated founders use Monte Carlo simulation. This technique runs your financial model thousands of times, each with slightly different inputs drawn from realistic probability distributions. Revenue growth might vary between 0% and 15% month-over-month. Churn might range from 2% to 6%. Hiring might be on schedule or delayed by two months.",
      "After 1,000 or more iterations, you get a distribution of outcomes rather than a single number. The P10 result (10th percentile) is your pessimistic case — only 10% of simulations produced a worse outcome. The P50 is the median. The P90 is optimistic. Together, these three numbers give you a confidence band: for example, your runway is somewhere between 8 months (P10) and 19 months (P90), with a median of 13 months.",
      "## Key Variables That Affect Runway",
      "Revenue growth rate is the single biggest lever. Even 5% monthly growth compounds dramatically — $30,000 in monthly revenue becomes $64,000 in 16 months at 5% growth. Customer churn works in the opposite direction, eroding your revenue base each month. Hiring pace is usually the largest controllable expense; each new hire adds $8,000 to $25,000+ in monthly burn depending on role and location. Payment terms and accounts receivable also matter — recognizing revenue is not the same as having cash in your account.",
